1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,652
Last time she called to say well done, and nothing depended on it.

2
00:00:06,102 --> 00:00:12,624
This time the job is not finished until the crew picks up and says so.

3
00:00:13,074 --> 00:00:16,552
And she is not alone on the line.

4
00:00:17,752 --> 00:00:19,926
Three changes to the job.

5
00:00:20,376 --> 00:00:22,115
The reward comes off.

6
00:00:22,565 --> 00:00:23,870
Then, reveal report_in.

7
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:29,537
And one more sentence, so the crew knows the pay comes later.

8
00:00:29,987 --> 00:00:32,161
Now the beat below it.

9
00:00:32,611 --> 00:00:35,654
I am replacing it with a quest.

10
00:00:36,104 --> 00:00:43,930
No Beat line: so it is in the quest list while it runs, and a beat is not.

11
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:47,859
An objective, which sends the crew to Comms.

12
00:00:48,309 --> 00:00:50,917
The reward, which has moved here.

13
00:00:51,367 --> 00:00:57,454
The same Action as before: Action works on any quest, the moment it starts.

14
00:00:57,904 --> 00:01:00,513
And look at what is missing.

15
00:01:00,963 --> 00:01:02,267
No Done when.

16
00:01:02,717 --> 00:01:05,761
Nothing in the game finishes this step.

17
00:01:06,211 --> 00:01:07,950
Only an answer can.

18
00:01:09,150 --> 00:01:11,324
She should be asking now.

19
00:01:11,774 --> 00:01:13,513
Two takes, both questions.

20
00:01:13,963 --> 00:01:16,137
And the answer: completes report_in.

21
00:01:16,587 --> 00:01:19,630
That is the word from last time.

22
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:23,124
It finishes the step and pays it.

23
00:01:23,574 --> 00:01:27,052
Now a mistake that looks like good manners.

24
00:01:27,502 --> 00:01:29,676
A second answer: not now.

25
00:01:30,126 --> 00:01:34,039
A crew that picks it has ended the call.

26
00:01:34,489 --> 00:01:42,750
It does not come back, and the step sits in the quest list for the rest of the game.

27
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:48,417
So the rule: every answer that ends this call finishes the step.

28
00:01:48,867 --> 00:01:53,215
The crew already has a way to say not now.

29
00:01:53,665 --> 00:01:55,839
It is the Back button.

30
00:01:57,039 --> 00:02:01,387
A call can have more than one person on it.

31
00:02:01,837 --> 00:02:05,750
First the person: a heading, a key, a face.

32
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:07,504
Now the scene.

33
00:02:07,954 --> 00:02:12,737
At quill starts her block, as it did in lecture three.

34
00:02:13,187 --> 00:02:16,230
At ives hands the call to him.

35
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:17,985
His own takes.

36
00:02:18,435 --> 00:02:25,826
The crew reads her, presses Continue, and reads him, under his name and his face. Four things.

37
00:02:26,276 --> 00:02:29,754
The call is still Quill's in the list.

38
00:02:30,204 --> 00:02:34,987
The line is the at sign and a key, nothing else.

39
00:02:35,437 --> 00:02:48,046
Write his name there, or a space, or a colon, and it stops being a block: his lines become more takes for her, and lint will not tell you.

40
00:02:48,496 --> 00:02:51,104
Each block has its own takes.

41
00:02:51,554 --> 00:02:55,033
And the answers come with the last block.

42
00:02:56,233 --> 00:03:01,450
You have several calls now, so here is how the list works.

43
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:03,204
Newest on top.

44
00:03:03,654 --> 00:03:10,611
A row is a name and a title, so every call you place wants a title.

45
00:03:11,061 --> 00:03:13,235
A call never times out.

46
00:03:13,685 --> 00:03:17,163
And one call is open at a time.

47
00:03:17,613 --> 00:03:22,830
If a call must not be buried, give its scene a priority.

48
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:24,585
A whole number.

49
00:03:25,035 --> 00:03:28,948
Higher sits above lower, however new the others are.

50
00:03:29,398 --> 00:03:35,920
I use it for the call a step is waiting on, and for nothing else.

51
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:38,859
Who does she call?

52
00:03:39,309 --> 00:03:40,613
Every player ship.

53
00:03:41,063 --> 00:03:44,541
With one ship, that is the whole story.

54
00:03:44,991 --> 00:03:51,513
With two, each ship gets its own copy of the call and answers for itself.

55
00:03:51,963 --> 00:03:56,746
But the job is shared: one yes starts it for everyone.

56
00:03:57,196 --> 00:04:02,848
Both ships get the report call, and the first to report is paid.

57
00:04:03,298 --> 00:04:06,341
Two things for you as the writer.

58
00:04:06,791 --> 00:04:08,530
This take says Artemis.

59
00:04:08,980 --> 00:04:11,154
The Intrepid reads it too.

60
00:04:11,604 --> 00:04:19,865
And look at the last row: a ship that says yes after the job is finished starts it again.

61
00:04:20,315 --> 00:04:27,272
So a job handed out by a call belongs in a mission flown by one ship.

62
00:04:27,722 --> 00:04:31,200
You will see Scope ship in the documentation.

63
00:04:31,650 --> 00:04:34,259
In this mission it changes nothing.

64
00:04:34,709 --> 00:04:36,013
Leave it shared.

65
00:04:37,213 --> 00:04:38,952
Nothing to type here.

66
00:04:39,402 --> 00:04:45,054
This is what the person at Comms can do with what you wrote.

67
00:04:45,504 --> 00:04:47,243
Leave it: it waits.

68
00:04:47,693 --> 00:04:52,476
Open it: the bridge sees it on the main screen too.

69
00:04:52,926 --> 00:04:59,013
Back: it goes back in the list and nothing in the story has changed.

70
00:04:59,463 --> 00:05:02,507
The dial moves where it is drawn.

71
00:05:02,957 --> 00:05:06,000
The Hails tab keeps every finished call.

72
00:05:06,450 --> 00:05:10,363
And the ship's log gets one line per call.

73
00:05:10,813 --> 00:05:14,291
So an answer is a decision made once.

74
00:05:14,741 --> 00:05:20,393
Everything else lets the crew put it off, or look back at it.

75
00:05:21,593 --> 00:05:22,593
Lint. Clean.

76
00:05:23,043 --> 00:05:29,130
A key that is not a quest: it tells you the answer does nothing.

77
00:05:29,580 --> 00:05:33,059
A voice that is nobody in the cast.

78
00:05:33,509 --> 00:05:36,117
A field it does not know.

79
00:05:36,567 --> 00:05:38,741
Now two it cannot see.

80
00:05:39,191 --> 00:05:44,409
His name where his key goes: clean, and his block is gone.

81
00:05:44,859 --> 00:05:50,076
And an answer that finishes nothing: clean, and the job never ends.

82
00:05:50,526 --> 00:05:53,135
Those two you check by eye.

83
00:05:53,585 --> 00:05:56,193
The page has the whole list.

84
00:05:57,393 --> 00:05:59,133
We will tag her.

85
00:05:59,583 --> 00:06:02,191
The job is running. Thirty seconds.

86
00:06:02,641 --> 00:06:04,380
Done, and not paid.

87
00:06:04,830 --> 00:06:12,657
The list says report to DS 1, and there is the call. Quill. Continue. Chief Ives. Now Back.

88
00:06:13,107 --> 00:06:16,585
The call is waiting, the step is waiting.

89
00:06:17,035 --> 00:06:20,513
Open it again: it starts with her. Continue.

90
00:06:20,963 --> 00:06:22,702
It is ours. Paid.

91
00:06:23,152 --> 00:06:25,326
One line in the log.

92
00:06:25,776 --> 00:06:30,124
And here, every call we have had, to read again.

93
00:06:31,324 --> 00:06:34,802
Your second call, the one your timer places.

94
00:06:35,252 --> 00:06:45,252
Turn its beat into a step the crew can see, make every ending finish it, and put a second voice on the line.

95
00:06:45,702 --> 00:06:53,093
Then break it on purpose, so you have seen what a call that finishes nothing looks like.

96
00:06:53,543 --> 00:06:54,848
Next time: reputation.
